<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Manage Enterprise Capacity -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Manage Enterprise Capacity">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Process"/>
<TMF_v1:hasDomain rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Business_Entity.Enterprise Domain"/>
<TMF_v1:hasVerticalGroup rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#VG."/>
<TMF_v1:BriefDescription>Manage Enterprise Capacity business activity establishes current and future enterprise ""resources"" (i.e. materials, staff, stock, supplies etc.) and assures they are sufficient to meet planned and forecasted business needs.</TMF_v1:BriefDescription>
<TMF_v1:MaturityLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</TMF_v1:MaturityLevel>
<TMF_v1:OriginalProcessIdentifier>X.X.X.X.X</TMF_v1:OriginalProcessIdentifier>
<TMF_v1:ProcessIdentifier>1.7.1.7</TMF_v1:ProcessIdentifier>
<TMF_v1:ProcessLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</TMF_v1:ProcessLevel>
<rdf:label>Manage Enterprise Capacity Enterprise Capacity Management business process establishes current and future enterprise "resources" (i.e. materials, staff, stock, supplies etc.) are sufficient to meet planned and forecasted business needs.
Enterprise Capacity Management business process support the organization in determining optimal availability for current and future business intents for use or consumption, and aligns the allocation of available and planned "resources" relationship between all value streams, such product management, service management and production resource management. Through this business process, maximum levels of output that an organization can sustain for production (to make a product or provide a service) are defined and managed in a continuum.
Enterprise Capacity Management process works closely with service level management to ensure that the business requirements for capacity are meet expectations of the enterprises performance goals. Enterprise Capacity Management also serves as a focal point for any capacity issues in the organization as it supports product, services and resources planning, availability and performance.
Examples of Enterprise Capacity Management include:
A telco that can support 200 million minutes of use across a 200 solar powered base stations with a 40 man network operations team and 1000 subscribers in a week.
A caf&#233; that can brew 1800 cups of coffee in a working day of 8 hours.
A call center scoped to field 7,000 calls per week with 120 agents in a 3 shift cycle.
An automobile production line that can assemble 250 trucks per month across 3 truck models.
A restaurant that is designed to support seating capacity that accommodates a maximum of 100 diners at its peak time per week.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Enterprise Capacity Planning -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Enterprise Capacity Planning">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Process"/>
<TMF_v1:hasDomain rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Business_Entity.Enterprise Domain"/>
<TMF_v1:hasVerticalGroup rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#VG."/>
<TMF_v1:BriefDescription>Enterprise Capacity Planning business process activity handle the tasks of balancing available "resources" against value streams need in order to meet the enterprises' demand management program.</TMF_v1:BriefDescription>
<TMF_v1:MaturityLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</TMF_v1:MaturityLevel>
<TMF_v1:OriginalProcessIdentifier>X.X.X.X.X.X</TMF_v1:OriginalProcessIdentifier>
<TMF_v1:ProcessIdentifier>1.7.1.7.1</TMF_v1:ProcessIdentifier>
<TMF_v1:ProcessLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</TMF_v1:ProcessLevel>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Capacity Planning Enterprise Capacity Planning business process activity handle the tasks of balancing available "resources" against value streams need in order to meet the enterprises' demand management program.
Enterprise Capacity Planning maintains a holistic view across the enterprise to ensure a match of supply with demand given all the resources available to the enterprise. It receives reports from all value streams (product/service and resource), and supports the overarching capacity management policies.
Enterprise Capacity Planning impacts on all the enterprise, it's projects, and measures and tracks the allocation and use of capacity based on well defined capacity management strategy.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Enterprise Capacity Management Requirement -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#Enterprise Capacity Management Requirement">
